4:30 p.m. on a Thursday afternoon in  Johannesburg South Africa it's 2008 and  when a myth of a global financial crisis  the largest one since the dot-com bubble  in the year 2000 rumors are swirling  about the global economy collapsing  about banks going bankrupt about people  losing their jobs I'm a commodity trader  in an investment bank and with only 30  minutes to go until the market closes I  can't wait because phones around me  ringing uncontrollably and computer  screens are flashing non-stop it's a  tense environment and one where  everybody's trying to manage their  profits to keep their jobs suddenly I  hear a voice the S&amp;P is falling where  can I sell 50 million dollars it's a  chaotic environment my heart beats  rapidly as I desperately tried to keep  my cool  finally the clock strikes 5:00 p.m. and  I breathe a sigh of relief as I manage  to leave the trading floor in one piece  as I sit in traffic on my way home I  feel anxious and struggle to focus as my  mind wanders off and after Synder for a  couple of minutes I noticed a  well-dressed middle-aged man trying to  cross a one-way road with no traffic  lights in sight and I think to myself  how strange what's he doing in the  middle of the road I observe as he makes  his way and as I get closer I knew that  he has a cane I notice is actually blind  so I merely jump out of my car offer my  assistance and ask him whether he needs  a ride anyway and to my surprise without  any hesitation he smiles at me  introduces himself as Eustace and gives  me his destination it wasn't a very long  ride but one through the interesting  conversation I asked the man  how do you manage to keep your composure  when in such a tense environment and  then he proceeded to tell me a story  about how he had not always been blind  and how losing his sight led him to pay  more attention to his surroundings he  told me they'd been a long journey for  him letting go of the resentment that he  had towards the world and towards the  circumstances that left him losing his  sight and through that process he  learned how to remain calm especially  when he felt lost or insecure I had to  learn not to focus on what was happening  now how else would I find my way that's  what he told me in that moment in that  very moment I came to a sudden  realization let go focus and listen you  see like that man I was stuck in my past  I let my anxiety take control of me as I  imagined worst case now for my future  based off previous economic downturns do  you ever feel as though you're the  person the middle the road with dozens  of cars honking at you  I suddenly do life's events often  approaches in a form of fast moving cars  in a busy road leaving us feeling lost  and insecure and these feelings are  amplified as ruminate on our past or on  uncertainties in our future how are we  able to navigate through the noise that  life presents unless we're better able  to manage our thoughts and focus on what  matters most to us as I gave more  thought to men's words I reflected upon  a concept called mindfulness mindfulness  is by no means a new topic in fact it's  been around for many many years it's  letting go of all thoughts in the past  and future and bringing increased  awareness to the present moment in our  everyday lives it's been shown to have a  number of benefits and is practiced by  some of the largest companies in the  world General Mills instituted a seven  week mindfulness program for its  employees the program aims to get its  leaders to develop their minds capacity  to pay attention both to themselves and  to others  was usually successful in fact 80% of  senior executives have reported improved  decision-making skills after taking the  class google has a now famous  mindfulness course called  search within yourself it's a rock-solid  business friendly mindfulness program  composed of practical ways to develop  three traits train your attention  develop self-awareness and create useful  mental habits the program was  oversubscribed it has a minimum waiting  time of no fewer than six months but how  does mindfulness make us better leaders  well there are many ways but what  Eustace learned that day was how to not  let his negative thoughts from his past  affect his present moment or his  perceptions of the future  better managing our thoughts extends to  a business context through a phenomenon  called sunk caused bias this is a bias  that a cousin one is invested so much  into a hopeless project that they cannot  bring themselves to stop for the fear of  losing all that was invested san'ko's  buys leads to future irrational business  decisions let's think about that for a  second imagine you enter an ice cream  shop and purchase an expensive yet  elaborate double chocolate peanut butter  ice cream half way through eating it you  start to feel sick do you throw the  remainder away or do you feel obliged to  finish it only because you spend so much  money on it already devouring that  remaining bit of ice cream won't bring  your money back in fact you might have  more to lose in the form of lost  pleasures or increased glucose levels  Suncoast buys the thinking trap and  practicing mindfulness reduces the  tendency to allow prior unrecoverable  costs to affect future decisions the  consequences of not better managing our  thoughts extends beyond the individual  or corporate level to a country level I  grew up in an apartheid trouble South  Africa where segregation was rife The  effect of this environment were damaging  to the emotional state of the population  where the consequence is still prevalent  today 21 years after democracy South  Africa's population is worried that its  future will end up like its past  leading to a mindset that promotes  social divide and impacts economic  performance ever since  reduce this to me be more mindful has  meant being able to let go focus and  listen it's the ability to let go of my  regrets in the past or my anxiety in the  future and to take a moment to focus and  listen truly listen to myself and to  others think about the last time you're  in conversation with someone that you  struggle to focus or listen to on what  to what they're saying maybe you are  thinking about what's in response  judging what they were saying or maybe  you're daydreaming altogether sometimes  or even too busy to listen down in a  thought needs or beliefs when was the  last time you listened to yourself fast  for a couple of months I'm back in the  trading floor but this time with a  different mindset let go focus and  listen I let go of the anxiety that I  had towards the uncontrollable events in  my life one tool that I found useful  well there are started reframing certain  situations as opportunities to grow  rather than as chances to fail so  instead of thinking a poor performance  today could lead to me losing my job I  thought I have a fantastic opportunity  to help my clients through difficult  economic times I found there was a lot  more energized to the task at hand and I  was actually better at my job I started  to focus more on the positives in my  life  including those little things that we  sometimes take for granted so for  example when driving home from work I  thought of a few things that I was  grateful for as opposed to replaying the  frustrating moments that I experienced  throughout the day this increased my  levels of happiness and made me a better  colleague and finally I listened to  myself as I reflected I realized that  throughout my life I'd been to results  focused especially on the trading floor  and I never really took the time to  fully appreciate what I learned  throughout the journey I started putting  mini pauses between my actions which  cleared my mind and allowed me to focus  more on what I was doing in that moment  I will likely never meet Eustace again  but I'll never forget the lesson that I  learned that day life really resembles  work on a clear or straight path but  rather looks like being blindfolded on a  busy road that's scary  so the next time you find yourself in  the middle of the road  we doesn't have cars honking at you  remember let go focus and listen listen  to those around you and listen to your  heart thank you
 

